First Author: Ma
Title: Developing approaches to incorporate donor-lung computed tomography images into machine learning models to predict severe primary graft dysfunction after lung transplantation
Source number: 33  ￼

Very short summary: This single-center study evaluated how donor-lung CT scans can be integrated with clinical data to predict severe primary graft dysfunction (PGD) after bilateral lung transplantation. Five key findings were: (1) 160 first-time bilateral lung transplant recipients with donor-lung CT scans were analyzed; (2) severe PGD occurred in 44 patients (27.5%); (3) among imaging-only models, a 3D ResNet approach performed best, with median AUROC 0.63 and AUPRC 0.48; (4) after reducing 98 clinical variables to 13 features, late fusion of CT and clinical data gave the best overall performance; and (5) the late-fusion multimodal model outperformed clinical-data-only prediction, achieving median AUROC 0.74 and AUPRC 0.61. Figures 1–2 and Tables 1–3 were directly used for this extraction.  ￼

